A||N||25||kornholio||Are they really that high maintenance??||||8
Z||000000||kornholio||10-15-1999||11:07 PM||||Here's the list of things I've had to repair/replace:<P>June: Replaced the brakes (to get the car certified)<BR>July: Replaced EGR tube (it broke!)<BR>August: Reverse did not work caused by dirty transmission fluid (previous owner's neglect)<BR>September 30: Replaced alternator<BR>October 14: Code 32, replaced the EGR valve.<BR>October 15: Replaced TPS (it got screwed up)<BR>October 15: Got the car back from the mechanic's and 3 hours later the front right brakes are making a grinding noise. Then I scrape the front end while coming out of a gas station and the brakes are making an even louder noise.  These are brand new (cheap) brakes.  I figure they should last longer than 4 months.<P>My question is:  Are Fiero's really this high maintenance?  Or was my car just neglected by the previous owner?  I've been told they are high maintenance, but this is crazy.  These problems every couple weeks are really pissing me off!||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||10-15-1999||11:24 PM||||I've only had my Fiero about 8 months now, but the only things I've ever had to replace abnormally are: thermostat and radiator cap.  Normal stuff is oil, filters, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2M4 Dale||10-16-1999||12:15 AM||||Got my Fiero in March,99. Both rear brake calipers and pads all around...four new shocks and a steering stabilizer...new tires...oil and lube...pcv valve...spark plugs...new belt tensioner and serpentine belt...air filter...Now my slave cylinder is bad...got one of those but am going to replace master clylinder and line too. Most of these things are a result of prior owners neglect. I fugure by spring I can have her mechanically sound. and then maybe some paint and a new sound system...So much for my $1000 initial investment. I LOVE IT ANYWAY||||reg||14
Z||000003||TOM||10-16-1999||01:22 AM||||Mine has been in the family since new. It has 170k and I dont regard it as high maintenance. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||10-16-1999||01:44 AM||||I bought my 88 GT two years ago and it had 91K at that time now it has 124 and the only thing I've replaced was a clutch an waterpump. It has to do with how a car is taken care of and how it is driven. I loved my GT so much I joined the Golden GAte Fieros here in the San Francisco Area and then since I was retired I opened a Fiero only shop.||||reg||10
Z||000005||WisconsinGT||10-16-1999||01:43 PM||||Yes they are high maintenance. But so is any 11+ year old car, with over 60,000 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GT||10-16-1999||06:31 PM||||I bought my GT back in April and the only thing I have replaced so far was the exhaust system, which is a common thing to do on a 12 year old car.  Other than that, superb!  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mwbackus||10-16-1999||06:41 PM||||I would guess that ones attitude towards the Fiero is quite related to the reasons behind purchasing one.  I purchased my Formula as a hobby project rather than a daily driver. If there were no improvements and repairs to make on this car, it would not be nearly as fun for me as it is. I have already spent more on upgrades and repairs than I did on the original purchase of the car, and I am sure that much more will be spent before my project is "done".  Hmmm.. I never thought about that concept before, perhaps it never really will be "done".  Oh well :-)<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||10-16-1999||06:59 PM||||When you consider the fact that my 85 SE is pushing 15 years old, it is amazing to me that it doesnt need more stuff done to it than it does. I have replaced shocks and struts, front wheel bearings, rear calipers etc. It has also been recently painted and I got some nice NOS seat covers from Tom to put in it. It still could use new front end bushings and probably ball joints, tie rod ends and a rack. But that is a small price to pay for the years of service this car has had. Yes, it is annoying to have so much stuff to do on one car, but if you wanted a "low maintenance" car, you can buy a new car, and have that big monthly payment! I cant afford that, so I will keep the old dog a little longer.||||reg||14
Z||000009||theogre||10-16-1999||07:40 PM||||you should expect to replace nearly all sensors on any car at around 100K miles. And a bunch of other things.<P>What the heck did you expect........ Car makers don't want cars to last forever...... other wise you don't buy new cars.....<P>As for griping about performance of cheap parts......... <P>Part prices are only partly a gauge of quality. I shop my parts around but I also watch the quality of the parts. I never "go cheap" on brakes unless I'm unloading the car. I may not always buy the name brand but I always look for the best part.<P>As for 4 months from brakes...... either they are crap, or the calipers are binding.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-16-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||kornholio||10-16-1999||08:09 PM||||It turns out that the brakes were not shot.  It was a shock mount that broke.||||reg||1
Z||000011||AL||10-16-1999||10:43 PM||||i bought my 86,5 fastback2 months ago,  knew it was old so i decided to do everything to it. i did the coolant system, which includes cleaning the radiator and thermistate, then i did my heads cleaned the plenum and changed all sensors, then i changed the spark plugs and wires plus module and cap and rotor, then i did my trany service. i paid 900 for my car and spent another 600 to change or replace what it needed and since then i haven't had a problem with it, it even passed california's bull shit test, so you see, i did all this but not in the order that i put it. but now i don't have to wory about the car giving me problems. an old car is like an old house if you realize that it is old and you have to put money into it, to make it look good, then once you start fixing it it will give you long lasting service, good luck and keep turning the wrenches, long live the fiero! ||||reg||14
Z||000012||DXR_DAD||10-16-1999||11:13 PM||||I'am with Cooter, my 86 has done me well. Most of all the replacement parts were put on because I wanted them. When I bought the car it had 66,000 and was clean in and out, but come on....11 - 15 year old cars??. They need some work!!||||reg||1
Z||000013||ggarsk||10-17-1999||12:01 AM||||My 84 SE cost me 350. It wasn't very well maintained I changed the oil(engine and tranny),all the filters,steering column,clutch pedal and some wiring. Plus all the cosmetic things that I have done. I probally have another 600 into it. It's still cheap for a car that is such a pleasure to drive. If you treat your car right,it will treat your right.||||reg||1
Z||000014||DJRice||10-18-1999||09:09 AM||||Ive had my 87 for about a year and a half and the only things Ive had to do are tires, brakes, water pump, heater core, and those stupid little things that hold your exhaust pipes up to the engine cradle.  <P>When I got the car, I did not intend it as a daily driver, but then my daily driver (a buick with almost 250,000 miles) decided it was time to quit, so the Fiero became my daily driver.  It did well, but I did go buy a Jeep Grand Cherokee.<P>Now I dont have a bunch of extra money to blow on my Fiero, Car Payments Suck!||||reg||1
Z||000015||mshill||10-18-1999||01:33 PM||||I have had my 86 SE for 3 months now.  It has been fairly well maintained but had 188K freeway miles on it.  I bought it knowing that I would replace every piece of rubber and every bearing in the entire car.  Here's what I have done so far:<P>Front Suspension bushing (a-arms, sway bar)<BR>Front Bearings<BR>Front Hubs/Rotors<BR>Front Brakes<BR>Front Shocks<P>Rebuilt Clutch Master<BR>Radiator (unexpected but not a surprise)<BR>Coolant fan sensor.<P>Fuel Pump (was very noisy)<BR>Fuel Filter<P>Rotor/Cap<BR>Ignition Wires and Plugs<BR>Distributor Pickup Coil (plastic housing disintegrated)<BR>Ignition Module (unexpected bummer)<P>Fuel/Temp guage<BR>125 MPH Speedometer<P>Catalytic Converter (unexpected, but not surprised)<P><BR>For the winter projects I have a complete engine rebuild, new clutch, tranny service, new struts, rear brakes, suspension and cradle bushings.  When all of this is done I start on the rebody and interior.  I'm just having a lot of fun.<P>After all of this, I boubt there will be a whole lot on the car that isn't new or rebuilt.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000016||Eddie||10-18-1999||05:38 PM||||Lets see I replaced the motor the TBI,WIRES THE ECM SPRINGS,SHOCKS,BUSHINGS,EXHAUST FROM HEADER TO MUFFLER.I COULD GO ON BUT I HAVE TO GET BACK TO WORK. THINK OF YOUR SELF TO BE LUCKY.	<BR>						EDDIE||||reg||1
Z||000017||stsmithgt||10-20-1999||02:09 PM||||WHAT? REPLACING PARTS?  I think we all have a list of parts we've replaced.  As said before, these are old cars.  I justify my parts replacment as a hobby of mine. So my interests have been: brake pads bearings and races, rotors, brake master cylinder, clutch master cylinder,tires,wheels,catyletic converter, muffler, fuel pump, egr pipe, idle controll sensor, egr sensor?, dog bone (polished up a bit), steering wheel($200.00 but it's COOL), HUF PUFF, AND THE LIST GOES ON. I have a list of things to do to almost as long.  I still enjoy my white 86 GT a lot.  Many items I plan on fixing are'nt mechanical.  For instance, it could use a paint job with a little more style.  Have fun you guys with your new or old hobby.||||reg||6
Z||000018||lowCG||10-20-1999||08:59 PM||||Pretty good time to find a car for hobby/rebuild purposes;there all racking up miles to where people don't want to drive them as much everyday.Like Al said,they're a bit like an old house and require some attention,nothing out of the ordinary though.<BR>I try to upgrade whatever I find that needs replacing,if there's any performance to be gained anyways.<BR>So far I've got a suspension that feels right at home at warp6,and am working on some brakes that'll give you bruises where the seatbelts go across your shoulder,next will be the 4 cam 3.4,all for the same or less $ than the rebuilt original junk. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Bugsy||10-22-1999||04:42 AM||||I've had my '86 SE since March, It had 72k on it. I bought it knowing it needed rubber on all 4 corners. Other than that I have an exhaust tick and have already replaced the catt converter...I'd drive it anywhere without a second thought||||reg||1
Z||000020||ChadMan||10-22-1999||12:40 PM||||Sounds like you're going through a painful "debugging" process, kornholio.  I went through a year and a half with my first Fiero (84) where it seemed like I was dropping $100 here and $200 there all the time - then once I got everything fixed, it gave me two and a half more years of trouble-free service.  Both my other Fieros have been relatively trouble-free.<P>Hang in there!  You're replacing stuff the previous owner neglected.  When you get it all fixed you'll have a trouble-free car.||||reg||1
Z||000021||trackerrrr||10-24-1999||12:53 AM||||I just got my 84 Sport Coupe at the end of Sept.  It has 309000 miles and was a one owner car.  The car was a freebie so I figured any maintenance would be worth it.  And how much have I spent to date?<P>$15 - spark plugs and wires<BR>$50 - hub assembly<BR>$25 - thermostat, cap, radiator cap, and<BR>___   antifreeze<BR>$90 - total!<P>The original owner was very meticulous about routine maintenance.  If your Fiero needs a bunch of work I'd wager the previous owner neglected or abused it!  My little ride is great!<P>||||reg||10
Z||000022||kornholio||10-24-1999||01:01 AM||||I just replaced the EGR solenoid.  Man was it out of shape...it was missing a vacuum line on it!  Now I don't get that code 32 anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000023||fastonpumpgas||10-24-1999||09:45 AM||||I also have an 85 frienza and I have replaced the battery, starter, vavle cover and gasket twice, alternator, tires like crazy.  So I wouldnt say they are any higher maintance than any other car that is 10 or 15 years old. I think because people drive them harder and more stuff wears out than some floor 4 putter.  (food for thought)||||reg||1
Z||000024||batboy||10-24-1999||10:47 AM||||Fastgas makes a good point. Many Fieros are driven fairly aggressively. Combine this with several years and many miles of maintenance neglect, then suddenly you have a sick car. Nurse it back to health, the Fiero is worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000025||88 Fiero||10-24-1999||11:01 AM||||Dont' feel frustated about replacing parts in your car kornholio...on the bright side your car will be running good again... I just started buying tools that I am going to need next year when I start tearing my fiero apart... I already got a set of Impact Tools and Air Hose.. I am already shopping for an Air Compressor...I think of it as a project car and I know my investment is well worth...||||reg||1
